    <h1>Facebook Privacy Policy: What to Disclose for Apps, Ads, and Pages</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Updated October 20, 2026</p>

    <p>Meta requires a privacy policy URL in three distinct places: the <strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong> field under <strong>App settings &gt; Basic</strong> in the App Dashboard, the <strong>Link URL</strong> field in the Privacy section of every Lead Ads instant form, and the <strong>Page Settings &gt; Page Info</strong> section for Facebook Pages that collect visitor data. Only the first two block you: an app cannot switch to Live mode, and an instant form cannot be published, while the field is empty.</p>

    <p>This guide covers where Meta asks for the URL across apps, ads, and Pages, what Facebook's Platform Terms and Business Tools Terms require your policy to disclose, the data each Graph API permission returns, the privacy policy field inside Lead Ads instant forms, the disclosure obligations that come with Meta Pixel and the Conversions API, and common reasons App Review rejects a privacy policy. If you need a finished policy without writing one from scratch, <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate a free privacy policy</a> and get a hosted URL you can paste into your App Dashboard or Lead Ads form.</p>

