    <h2 id="where-meta-asks">Where Meta Asks for a Privacy Policy URL</h2>

    <p>Inside the App Dashboard you enter the URL once; three later checkpoints re-read it.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>App settings &gt; Basic.</strong> Two fields matter here. <code>Privacy Policy URL</code> sits below <code>App Domains</code>, beside <code>Terms of Service URL</code>; Platform Terms clause 4.a requires your policy URL to be disclosed in this field. Directly beneath it, <code>User data deletion</code> takes either a <code>Data Deletion Instructions URL</code> (a page telling users how to request deletion) or a <code>Data Deletion Callback URL</code> (an endpoint Meta calls when a user removes your app). Leave the privacy field blank and the Development-to-Live toggle refuses to switch, with an error pointing back to this page. Run the URL through the <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/" rel="nofollow">Sharing Debugger</a> first: anything other than a 200 response code fails review.</li>

      <li><strong>App Review submission.</strong> When you submit permissions or features for App Review at <code>App Dashboard &gt; App Review &gt; Permissions and Features</code>, Meta's review team visits your privacy policy URL as part of their evaluation. The review checks that the policy matches the data your app actually accesses through the permissions you requested. A policy that does not mention the specific permissions under review will be rejected.</li>

      <li><strong>Business Verification.</strong> Apps requesting advanced access to certain permissions must complete Business Verification through the <code>App Dashboard &gt; Settings &gt; Advanced &gt; Business Verification</code> section. The verification process requires a legal entity with a privacy policy URL that matches the business identity. The policy must be hosted on a domain the business controls or on a verified website.</li>

      <li><strong>Data Use Checkup.</strong> Meta periodically requires apps to complete a Data Use Checkup through a banner notification in the App Dashboard. The checkup asks developers to re-confirm how they use each approved permission and verifies that the privacy policy URL is still active. Apps that fail the checkup or let it expire have their permissions revoked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="platform-terms">What Meta's Platform Terms Require the Policy to Disclose</h2>

    <p>Section 4 of Meta's <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/terms/" rel="nofollow">Platform Terms</a> is titled "Privacy Policy." Clause 4.a makes the URL mandatory: "You will provide, maintain, and comply with a privacy policy that is available through an active, publicly available, easily accessible (including by our crawlers), and non-geoblocked URL. This URL must also be disclosed in the privacy policy field in the settings of your App Dashboard." Clause 4.b sets the content: the policy "must clearly explain what data you are Processing, how you are Processing it, the purposes for which you are Processing it, and how Users may request deletion of that data." Meta's <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/development/terms-and-policies/privacy-policy/" rel="nofollow">Privacy Policy Expectations</a> page adds that it "must be your own privacy policy, and not the policy of another company."</p>

    <p>The policy must disclose:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>What data is collected.</strong> Every category of information your app collects from or about users, whether obtained directly through Facebook's APIs, through third-party SDKs, or gathered independently (browser information, IP addresses, device identifiers, server logs). If your app requests the <code>email</code> permission, your policy must state that you collect email addresses from Facebook. If you request <code>user_birthday</code>, you must disclose that you collect date of birth.</li>

      <li><strong>How the data is used.</strong> Each specific purpose for processing the data. Meta expects concrete descriptions -- "to personalize content recommendations," "to send weekly summary emails," "to match users with relevant groups" -- not vague catch-alls like "to improve our services." The purpose must match what you described in your App Review submission.</li>

      <li><strong>How users can request deletion.</strong> A working method for users to request that their data be deleted. This can be an email address, a contact form, or, for apps that support it, Meta's <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/development/create-an-app/app-dashboard/data-deletion-callback" rel="nofollow">Data Deletion Request Callback</a>. The URL or contact method you provide must be active and monitored -- Meta's review team tests it. If your app implements the Data Deletion Callback, enter it under <code>App settings &gt; Basic &gt; User data deletion</code> as a callback URL or a data deletion instructions URL.</li>

      <li><strong>Third-party sharing.</strong> Whether data obtained through Facebook's APIs is shared with any third party, and if so, which parties and for what purposes. Meta's Platform Terms prohibit transferring data received through their APIs to any data broker, ad network, or data reseller, even if your policy discloses it.</li>

      <li><strong>Data retention.</strong> How long you keep data obtained through the APIs. Platform Terms clause 3.d requires deletion when a user asks, when they no longer have an account with you, and when you stop operating the product that collected the data. Meta signals app removals to the <code>Deauthorize Callback URL</code> set under Facebook Login settings.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Clause 4.c then limits you to processing Platform Data "as clearly described in your privacy policy," so data missing from the list above is data you may not use. <a href="/resources/how-to-write-privacy-policy-for-app/">Writing a complete privacy policy for any app</a> starts with auditing which data your app actually touches.</p>

