    <p>What each permission obliges the policy to say: for <code>public_profile</code>, that you store the app-scoped user ID, name, and profile picture; for <code>email</code>, that you receive the account's primary email and what you send to it; for <code>user_photos</code>, which photos you read, whether you copy them to your own storage, and for how long; for <code>pages_show_list</code>, that you read the Pages a person manages (Meta's submission form asks for "specific examples of why your app needs to access the list of pages that a user owns"); for <code>ads_management</code>, that you read and change ad accounts on the user's behalf, which also requires Business Verification. A permission with no matching feature in your screencast is rejected even when the policy covers it. Generate the data-collection disclosures for any third-party SDKs your app bundles with the <a href="/tools/sdk-disclosure-generator/">SDK disclosure generator</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="pages-lead-ads">Facebook Pages and Lead Ads Privacy Requirements</h2>

    <p>Neither surface below goes through App Review; Ads Manager and Page settings enforce them instead.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Facebook Pages.</strong> Any Page that collects information from visitors -- through messaging, Lead Ads, or Shop features -- must link to a privacy policy. For Pages managed through a third-party app, the app's privacy policy must also disclose that it accesses Page data. The <code>pages_read_engagement</code> scope gives access to follower PSIDs (Page-Scoped User IDs) and profile pictures, which are personal data under GDPR even though they differ from the user's global Facebook ID.</li>

      <li><strong>Lead Ads.</strong> Every instant form needs a policy URL before it publishes, and the policy must name each field the form collects. The form builder steps are in the next section.</li>

      <li><strong>Custom Audiences.</strong> If your app uploads customer lists to create Custom Audiences (via <code>ads_management</code> or the Marketing API), your privacy policy must disclose that you share user data with Meta for advertising purposes. This includes hashed email addresses, phone numbers, and any other identifiers used for matching.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="lead-ads-instant-forms">Privacy Policy URL in Meta Lead Ads Instant Forms</h2>

    <p>Every Lead Ad campaign in Meta Ads Manager requires an instant form, and every instant form requires a privacy policy URL before it can be published. This requirement applies to anyone running lead generation ads on Facebook or Instagram -- you do not need a Facebook app or App Review to encounter it.</p>

    <h3>Where the field sits in Ads Manager</h3>

    <p>In <strong>Meta Ads Manager</strong>, choose the <strong>Leads</strong> objective, set the ad set's conversion location to <strong>Instant forms</strong>, then click <strong>Create form</strong> at the ad level. The editor runs in this order: <strong>Form type</strong>, <strong>Intro</strong>, <strong>Questions</strong>, <strong>Privacy</strong>, <strong>Message for leads</strong>. The Privacy section holds two required fields, <code>Link text</code> (the words shown on the form) and <code>Link URL</code> (your policy), plus an optional custom disclaimer for consent wording such as a marketing opt-in. The form will not publish until <code>Link URL</code> holds an HTTPS address. On the rendered form the link appears on the final review screen, next to Meta's notice that the info is sent to your business, before the user taps <strong>Submit</strong>.</p>

    <h3>What Meta rejects</h3>

    <p>Meta validates the privacy policy URL when you save the instant form. The URL must return an HTTP 200 response over HTTPS. A URL that redirects through a login wall, returns a 404, points to <code>localhost</code>, or loads a page that is not recognizably a privacy policy will cause the form to fail validation. Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/legal/lead_ads_terms" rel="nofollow">Lead Ads Terms</a> require that the linked policy be your own (not a third party's boilerplate) and that it accurately describe how you handle the data collected through the form.</p>

    <h3>What the policy must disclose about lead data</h3>

    <p>The instant form collects exactly the fields you configure: standard fields such as <strong>Full Name</strong>, <strong>Email Address</strong>, <strong>Phone Number</strong>, <strong>Job Title</strong>, <strong>Company Name</strong>, and <strong>Work Email</strong>, plus any <strong>Custom Questions</strong> you add (short-answer, multiple-choice, or conditional). Your privacy policy must list each category of data the form collects and explain:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Purpose of collection.</strong> Why you are collecting each field -- for example, "to send a quote by email" or "to schedule a consultation call." Vague purposes such as "to improve our services" do not satisfy Meta's Lead Ads Terms.</li>
      <li><strong>Third-party sharing.</strong> Whether the lead data is sent to a CRM (Salesforce, HubSpot, Zoho), an email marketing platform (Mailchimp, ActiveCampaign), or any other downstream service. Name each recipient. If you use Meta's <strong>CRM Integration</strong> in the instant form settings to push leads directly to a third-party system, that data flow must be disclosed.</li>
      <li><strong>Data retention and deletion.</strong> How long you keep the lead data and how someone who submitted the form can request deletion. Lead data downloaded from Ads Manager or received via the Leads API does not expire automatically on your end -- you are responsible for the retention lifecycle once it leaves Meta.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If you also use the <strong>Meta Pixel</strong> or <strong>Conversions API</strong> to send conversion events back to Meta when a lead converts (for example, firing a <code>Lead</code> event or a <code>Purchase</code> event), those data flows create a separate disclosure obligation covered in the next section. To generate a privacy policy that covers both Lead Ads form fields and Pixel event data, <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">use the free privacy policy generator</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="page-pixel-capi">Privacy Policy for a Facebook Page and Meta Pixel / Conversions API</h2>

    <p>Running a Facebook Page, installing the Meta Pixel on your website, or sending server-side events through the Conversions API each triggers a privacy policy obligation under Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/legal/terms/businesstools" rel="nofollow">Business Tools Terms</a> (formerly "Facebook Business Tools Terms"). These requirements are separate from App Review and apply to marketers, publishers, and business owners who never build a Facebook app.</p>

    <h3>Facebook Page privacy policy requirements</h3>

    <p>Any Facebook Page that collects information from visitors must make a privacy policy accessible. The collection happens through several built-in Page features:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Messenger conversations.</strong> When a visitor messages your Page, Meta stores the conversation and makes the sender's name, profile picture, and Page-Scoped User ID (PSID) available to you through the <strong>Page Inbox</strong> or the <strong>Messenger Platform API</strong>. Your policy must disclose that you collect and store messages and sender identity data from Facebook Messenger.</li>
      <li><strong>Page Insights.</strong> Meta provides Page administrators with aggregated demographics and engagement data through <strong>Page Insights</strong> (accessible at <code>Page &gt; Insights</code> or via the <code>read_insights</code> Graph API permission). Under the GDPR, Page administrators and Meta are joint controllers for the processing that produces Page Insights data, as established by the Court of Justice of the European Union in <em>Wirtschaftsakademie</em> (Case C-210/16). Your policy should state that you receive aggregated analytics about Page visitors from Meta.</li>
      <li><strong>Shop and checkout.</strong> Pages with an active <strong>Facebook Shop</strong> collect order information (name, shipping address, email, payment method) through Meta's Commerce Manager. Each data point collected at checkout must appear in your privacy policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>You can add your privacy policy URL in <strong>Page Settings &gt; Page Info &gt; Privacy Policy</strong> (under the "Additional Information" section). While Meta does not block a Page from operating without this field, running Lead Ads, Commerce features, or Messenger automations from a Page without a linked policy violates Meta's terms and exposes you to enforcement action.</p>

    <h3>Meta Pixel disclosure obligations</h3>

    <p>The <strong>Meta Pixel</strong> (formerly Facebook Pixel) is a JavaScript snippet you install on your website that fires HTTP requests to <code>facebook.com/tr/</code> whenever a visitor loads a page or triggers an event. Section 2 of Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/legal/terms/businesstools" rel="nofollow">Business Tools Terms</a> requires you to provide clear and prominent notice to your website visitors about the data you collect and share with Meta through Business Tools, and to obtain any consent required by applicable law before using them.</p>

    <p>The Pixel collects and transmits the following data to Meta with every event fire:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Browser and device data:</strong> IP address, User Agent string, screen resolution, browser language, and time zone.</li>
      <li><strong>Page data:</strong> the full URL of the page viewed, the referring URL, and the page title.</li>
      <li><strong>Facebook cookies:</strong> the <code>_fbp</code> first-party cookie (a browser identifier set by the Pixel) and the <code>_fbc</code> cookie (which stores the click identifier from an ad click, <code>fbclid</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>Event parameters:</strong> for standard events like <code>ViewContent</code>, <code>AddToCart</code>, <code>Purchase</code>, or <code>Lead</code>, the Pixel sends event-specific parameters you configure -- product IDs, values, currencies, content categories.</li>
      <li><strong>Advanced Matching data (if enabled):</strong> when you turn on <strong>Automatic Advanced Matching</strong> in <strong>Events Manager &gt; Settings &gt; Advanced Matching</strong>, the Pixel reads form fields on your page and hashes and sends personal data including email address, phone number, first name, last name, city, state, zip code, date of birth, and gender to Meta for identity resolution.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Your privacy policy must disclose that you use the Meta Pixel, name the categories of data listed above that apply to your implementation, state that this data is shared with Meta for ad measurement and optimization, and explain how visitors can opt out. Point users to Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/adpreferences" rel="nofollow">Ad Preferences</a> page and to your own cookie consent mechanism if you operate one.</p>

    <h3>Conversions API disclosure obligations</h3>

    <p>The <strong>Conversions API</strong> (CAPI, formerly Server-Side API) sends event data from your server directly to Meta's <code>graph.facebook.com</code> endpoint, bypassing the browser. You configure it in <strong>Events Manager &gt; Settings &gt; Conversions API</strong> or through a partner integration (Shopify, WordPress, Google Tag Manager server container). The data you send through CAPI is structurally the same as what the Pixel collects -- event name, event parameters, and user data parameters for matching -- but because it is server-to-server, it can include data the browser Pixel never sees, such as CRM records, offline purchase data, or call center outcomes.</p>

    <p>Meta's Business Tools Terms apply to CAPI identically to the Pixel. Your privacy policy must disclose:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>That you send event data from your server to Meta for ad measurement, optimization, and audience building.</li>
      <li>The categories of user data parameters you include in CAPI payloads -- typically hashed email (<code>em</code>), hashed phone (<code>ph</code>), IP address (<code>client_ip_address</code>), User Agent (<code>client_user_agent</code>), and the Facebook click ID (<code>fbc</code>) and browser ID (<code>fbp</code>).</li>
      <li>Whether you send offline events (in-store purchases, phone orders) through CAPI, and if so, that customer data from those transactions is shared with Meta in hashed form.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If you run both Meta Pixel and Conversions API (the setup Meta calls <strong>redundant event tracking</strong> for deduplication), a single disclosure section in your privacy policy can cover both, as long as it names both the client-side Pixel and the server-side API and describes the data each transmits. <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">Generate a free privacy policy</a> that includes Meta Business Tools disclosures alongside your other data practices.</p>

    <h2 id="rejection-reasons">Why App Review Rejects a Privacy Policy URL</h2>

    <p>Rejection notes appear per permission under <code>App Review &gt; Requests</code>. For privacy policy problems they trace back to the clauses quoted above:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>URL not reachable by Meta's crawler.</strong> Clause 4.a requires a URL that is "easily accessible (including by our crawlers), and non-geoblocked." Login walls, VPN-only staging hosts, Cloudflare bot challenges, country blocks, and HTTP-only pages all fail. Meta's Privacy Policy Expectations page warns: "Broken privacy policy links are considered violations and are subject to enforcement." Check the response code in the <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/" rel="nofollow">Sharing Debugger</a> before submitting.</li>

      <li><strong>Page is not clearly a privacy policy.</strong> The URL points to a homepage, About page, or terms of service with a privacy paragraph buried inside. The expectations page says "the linked page must be clearly marked as a privacy policy."</li>

      <li><strong>Someone else's policy.</strong> Linking to Meta's own Privacy Policy, an app store listing, or a template vendor's policy fails the "must be your own privacy policy" rule.</li>

      <li><strong>No deletion route.</strong> Clause 4.b requires the policy to explain "how Users may request deletion of that data," and the <code>User data deletion</code> field must hold a working instructions URL or callback. A contact page that never mentions deletion gets cited here.</li>

      <li><strong>Permission data missing from the policy.</strong> A template saying "we collect personal information" without naming the Facebook data each requested permission returns (see the table above) is rejected for that permission, even if the others pass.</li>

      <li><strong>Reviewer cannot test the app.</strong> Not a policy defect, but it fails the whole submission. Provide non-expiring test credentials without two-factor prompts, plus step-by-step instructions in the submission notes.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Fix the cited item, keep the same URL, and resubmit from <code>App Review &gt; Requests</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="disclosure-template">Copy-Ready Facebook App Disclosure Block</h2>

    <p>Add this block to your existing privacy policy to cover your app's Facebook integration. Replace the bracketed fields with your actual data practices and remove any sections that do not apply.</p>

    <div class="copy-block">
      <div class="copy-block-header">
        <span class="copy-block-label">Facebook App Privacy Disclosure &mdash; ready to copy</span>
      </div>
      <div class="copy-block-body" id="disclosure-text">
<p><strong>Facebook Integration</strong></p>

<p>Our application integrates with Facebook's platform through the Graph API and Facebook Login. This section describes the data we receive through these integrations.</p>

<p><strong>Data We Collect Through Facebook Login</strong></p>
<p>When you log in with Facebook, we receive and store the following information from your Facebook account, based on the permissions you grant:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Basic profile information</em> (granted automatically) &mdash; your user ID, first name, last name, and profile picture URL.</li>
<li><em>Email address</em> [if email permission is requested] &mdash; the primary email address associated with your Facebook account.</li>
<li>[Add additional permissions as applicable, e.g.: <em>Date of birth</em> (user_birthday permission), <em>Friends list</em> (user_friends permission &mdash; limited to friends who also use this app).]</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>How We Use Facebook Data</strong></p>
<p>We use the data received through Facebook's APIs to: [describe each specific purpose, e.g., authenticate your identity and create your account / personalize your experience / display your profile information within the app]. We do not use Facebook data for advertising, sell it to third parties, or share it with data brokers.</p>

<p><strong>Data Deletion</strong></p>
<p>You may request deletion of all data we received through Facebook by [contacting us at your-email@example.com / using the deletion form at (URL) / revoking the app's access in your Facebook Settings &gt; Apps and Websites, which triggers our automated deletion process via Meta's Data Deletion Request Callback]. We process deletion requests within [number] days.</p>

<p><strong>Third-Party Services Receiving Facebook Data</strong></p>
<p>[Name each service and what it receives, e.g.: <strong>AWS</strong> hosts our servers and stores your Facebook profile data. <strong>Sentry</strong> receives error logs that may include your user ID. No other third party receives data obtained through Facebook's APIs.]</p>

<p><strong>Data Retention</strong></p>
<p>Data received through Facebook's APIs is retained for [number] days while your account is active. When you delete your account or revoke our app's access through Facebook, we delete all Facebook-obtained data within [number] days.</p>
      </div>
      <button class="copy-btn" id="copy-disclosure-btn" type="button">Copy to clipboard</button>
    </div>

    <p>Need a complete privacy policy rather than just the Facebook section? <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">Use the free privacy policy generator</a> to build a full policy that covers GDPR, CCPA, and app store requirements, then host it at a permanent URL you can paste into your App Dashboard.</p>

    <RelatedGuides currentSlug="resources/facebook-app-privacy-policy" />

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Does every Facebook app need a privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Every app that goes through App Review or is made available to users beyond the app's development team must have a privacy policy URL in the App Dashboard. Apps used only by people with a role on the app (administrator, developer, tester) can function without one, but any public-facing app cannot pass App Review without it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Where do I add the privacy policy URL in the Facebook App Dashboard?</h3>
        <p>Go to <code>App settings &gt; Basic</code> in the App Dashboard and enter the URL in the <strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong> field. The field appears below the <code>App Domains</code> field. The URL must load over HTTPS, return an HTTP 200 response, and be accessible without authentication. After saving, use the <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/" rel="nofollow">Sharing Debugger</a> to verify Meta can reach the page.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What does Meta check during App Review for the privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>The review team visits the privacy policy URL and verifies that the page is accessible, loads over HTTPS, is clearly labeled as a privacy policy, and describes the app's data practices in enough detail to cover each requested permission. The policy must explain what data is collected, how it is used, how users can request deletion, and whether data is shared with third parties. Generic policies that do not mention Facebook-specific data are rejected.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What data does the public_profile permission give access to?</h3>
        <p>The <code>public_profile</code> permission is granted automatically to every app and provides access to the user's ID, first name, last name, profile picture URL, profile link, name format, and short name. It does not include email, birthday, gender, location, or friends list -- those require separate permissions and separate App Review approval.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can I use Google Docs or Notion to host my privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>It is not recommended. Google Docs and Notion pages can change their sharing settings, move behind authentication, or have URLs that redirect unpredictably. Meta's crawler needs a stable HTTPS URL that consistently returns an HTTP 200 response. Use a dedicated page on your own website, or <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate and host a free privacy policy</a> with a permanent URL.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How long does App Review take after fixing a privacy policy rejection?</h3>
        <p>Re-review after fixing a privacy policy issue typically takes 1 to 5 business days. Fix the specific issue cited in the rejection email, update the policy at the same URL, and resubmit through <code>App Dashboard &gt; App Review &gt; Requests</code>. Do not change the URL unless the original was unreachable -- the review team recognizes resubmissions to the same URL.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need a separate privacy policy for Lead Ads?</h3>
        <p>You do not need a separate document, but your existing privacy policy must cover the data collected through Lead Ad forms. When creating a Lead Ad form in Ads Manager, the <code>Privacy Policy</code> field requires a URL, and the same policy can serve both the app and the ad forms as long as it discloses the lead form data fields (name, email, phone, job title, or whatever custom fields you defined).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What happens if my privacy policy URL breaks after App Review approval?</h3>
        <p>Meta periodically re-checks privacy policy URLs through the Data Use Checkup and automated crawls. A broken URL -- one that returns a 404, 500, or redirects to a login page -- can result in your app's permissions being suspended. The App Dashboard will display a warning, and you will receive an email notification. Fix the URL promptly and complete any required Data Use Checkup to restore permissions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need a privacy policy to run Facebook or Instagram ads?</h3>
        <p>If you run Lead Ads, yes -- Meta Ads Manager requires a privacy policy URL in the <code>Link URL</code> field of every instant form's Privacy section, and the form cannot be published without one. For non-lead ad formats (traffic, conversions, engagement), Meta does not block the ad from running without a linked policy, but if you use the Meta Pixel or Conversions API on your landing page, Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/legal/terms/businesstools" rel="nofollow">Business Tools Terms</a> require you to disclose the data collection in your privacy policy and obtain any consent required by applicable law.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What must my privacy policy say about Meta Pixel?</h3>
        <p>Your policy must state that you use the Meta Pixel on your website, that it collects browser data (IP address, User Agent, page URLs), sets the <code>_fbp</code> and <code>_fbc</code> cookies, and shares this data with Meta for ad measurement and optimization. If you enable Automatic Advanced Matching in Events Manager, you must also disclose that hashed personal data (email, phone, name) is sent to Meta. Point users to Meta's <a href="https://www.facebook.com/adpreferences" rel="nofollow">Ad Preferences</a> page for opt-out.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
